# Worker: Identity, Roles & Admin

## Goal (Master-aligned)

Secure local JWT auth, Google OAuth, and roles so investigators can reach **Intake → Compare → Report**. Auth enables the IR workflow; it does not redefine product UX or statute authority.

## Role boundary

**Own:** JWT/session, Google OAuth, roles (admin/editor/viewer), permissions checks, admin users/inbox/audit, login UI, `frontend/src/components/RequireAuth.tsx`.

**Do not own:** IR drafting/quotes, statute corpus, stepper/Compare rails, PII gate logic, case ownership workflows, Launch/ports (env wiring only via escalate).

## Invariants

- Never commit secrets, client secrets, real SMTP credentials, or `backend/.env`
- Bootstrap admin is **local-dev only** — do not bake production secrets into the repo
- OAuth redirect must match `APP_PUBLIC_URL` / local UI origin
- Auth gates protect IR access; they must not reshape Intake → Compare → Report or revive analyzer-first UX
- Role checks stay consistent API ↔ UI

## Anti-goals

- Redesigning product navigation or IR screens under an “auth redesign”
- Hardening prod by committing credentials or embedding bootstrap admin for production
- Expanding admin surfaces into PII-heavy case content beyond existing audit/support design
- Treating login/roles as the product north star over Investigation Report drafting

## Success criteria

- Unauthenticated users cannot reach protected IR routes (`RequireAuth` + API auth)
- Roles enforce intended admin vs editor vs viewer capabilities without breaking IR flow
- OAuth/local login works against documented env; no secrets in git
- Admin users/inbox/audit remain usable without leaking PII beyond existing design

## Escalation map

| Signal | Escalate to |
|--------|-------------|
| PII in support tickets / admin views | Privacy & PII Gate |
| Local OAuth env, ports, Launch/debug | Local DevEx & Launch |
| Case ownership / review status gates | Case Workspace & Review |
| Login blocking stepper or Compare UX | Intake UX & Statute Selection |

